1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Every counting question is built from two moves. The sum rule: if a job can be done one way OR another, add the counts.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The product rule: if the job needs one thing AND then another, multiply the counts.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Draw the product as a grid. Three rows and two columns, and every one of the six cells is one outfit — the grid is the multiplication.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The connective decides the operation: or means add, and means multiply. But a plain sum needs the alternatives to be disjoint.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Two questions decide almost every counting formula: does the order of the chosen items matter, and may an item be chosen more than once?

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Order matters and repetition is allowed: n to the power r, one independent choice of n things at each of r stages.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Order matters and repetition is banned: n times n minus one down to n minus r plus one, written P of n comma r.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Order does not matter: C of n comma r when repetition is banned, and C of n plus r minus one comma r when it is allowed.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Take one combination — the set A, B, C — and write down every order of it. There are six.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Collapse the six into one. Permutations of r things from n are exactly r factorial times the combinations.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
So C of n comma r is n factorial over r factorial times n minus r factorial, and the binomial row adds to two to the n.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Whenever a count comes out too big, find the thing you counted several times and divide by how many times.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Four people at a round table: seat them in a row and there are twenty-four orders, but the table can be rotated.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Twenty-four divided by four is six, and the general answer is n minus one factorial.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,000
The same move handles a word with a repeated letter. LILAC has five letters but only sixty distinct arrangements, not a hundred and twenty.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A necklace or a bracelet has more symmetry than a table: reflections count too, so the divisor is larger.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Five identical sweets into three named children. Write the five as stars, and separate the three shares with two bars.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Every pattern of five stars and two bars is one distribution, and every distribution is one pattern. So count the patterns.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Seven positions choose two is twenty-one, and in general n plus r minus one choose r. The bars are the thing you choose.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Change the condition and the pattern changes. Each bin at least one means no two bars touch, so reserve one star per bin first.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Two sets that overlap. Adding their sizes counts the overlap twice, so subtract it once.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Three sets: add the singles, subtract the pairs, add the triple back. The signs alternate, and the last term is not a mistake.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Between 1 and 500 there are 166 multiples of 3, 100 of 5 and 71 of 7. Add: 337. Subtract the pairs: 267.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The answer is 271. Inclusion and exclusion is also the engine behind counting surjections and derangements, later in this lesson.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Five pigeons into four holes. However you place them, some hole holds at least two. That is not a probability; it is forced.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Generalised: N pigeons in k holes force some hole to hold at least the ceiling of N over k.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:23,000
A deck has four suits. Two cards per suit is eight cards with no three of a kind, so nine cards guarantee three of one suit.

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The direction of the inequality is the whole question. More objects than holes forces a collision; fewer objects proves nothing.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
A function from a four-element set to a three-element set is four independent choices, one image per element. That is eighty-one.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
An injection forbids repetition, so the count is P of n comma r — and it is zero as soon as the domain is larger than the codomain.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,000
A surjection must hit all three images, which is an "at least one" condition. Inclusion and exclusion gives 81 minus 48 plus 3.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Only the surjection count needs the repair. All functions is a product, injections a falling product, and surjections an alternating sum.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
A derangement is an arrangement that leaves nothing in its original place. Three items have six arrangements and two derangements.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Count the complement instead: six total, minus those fixing at least one position. Subtract the singles, add the pairs, subtract the triple.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:23,000
The sequence runs 0, 1, 2, 9, 44, 265, and it is always the nearest integer to n factorial over e.

36
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
LILAC: sixty distinct arrangements, and twelve leave no letter in its own place. The repeated L is handled before the derangement count.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
One checklist, in order: are the objects distinct or identical, does order matter, may items repeat, and is there a condition?

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Trap one: multiplying when the stages are not independent. Trap two: forgetting to divide by the internal order.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Trap three: adding overlapping alternatives. Trap four: reading a pigeonhole guarantee as an exact count.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Check every answer on the smallest case you can list by hand. That single habit catches all four traps.

